\f1\fs22 A few nights later, Sarah returned. All the unsaid things remained unsaid, and a new detente made the spaces between them safe. After dinner, they were playing 
\f2\i Xtreme Fighting Porn - The Game
\f1\i0  on Chanta\'92s Paystation LXIV, when Sarah jumped as something clattered in the fire escape. It sounded to her like a metal bucket falling down a dozen flights of stairs. Reflexes kicked in: her hand landed on Tony\'92s thigh, making him wince over his burns.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93What was that?\'94 Sarah hissed.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93A metal bucket falling down a dozen flights of stairs,\'94 Chanta muttered, moving her hand. Then: \'93Someone\'92s coming down the fire escape, and they didn\'92t know to avoid the bucket. We gotta go.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He swung the front door open and hustled Sarah into the narrow corridor. Halfway to the elevator, a huge, square-headed shadow blocked their escape.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You!\'94 the shadow spat.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You!\'94 Recognising the agent from Travis Teh\'92s office, Sarah grabbed Chanta and back-pedalled to his apartment.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Once they were inside, Chanta slammed his back against the front door as if that would stop anything. \'93What\'92s that ape want with you?\'94 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Me? He wants you, you idiot!\'94 Sarah stomped around, inspecting the windows for an escape or a rainbow bridge to anywhere. \'93That\'92s the PAPER agent I warned you about.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta screwed his face up, lost.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah stopped pacing. \'93Before the bombing! I came to warn you, remember?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>To Chanta, before the bombing seemed a geological epoch ago.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Three fist-sized thumps shook the door. \'93You will return what you stole!\'94 Blunt, relentless.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah whipped a tablet from her bag and dashed to the apartment\'92s kitchen console, jabbing at the screen. \'93Come on,\'94 she muttered at the tablet, working frantic gestures. \'93He\'92ll be requesting your door unlock codes. If I can\'92t subvert him in time\'85\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta\'92s heart ricocheted. He hovered, a useless witness, as Sarah fought the lock.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The front door gave a thunderous, final click.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Tax me dead,\'94 Sarah muttered.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Agent Jones barrelled inside. In a blink, he seized Chanta by the throat. With the easy momentum of an apex predator, he lifted Chanta clear, shunting him choking and flailing over the coffee table and into the couch, which slammed against the wall.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You will give me the disc,\'94 Agent Jones rumbled, calmly smoothing his red tie.\

\f2\i <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Disc? Wasn\'92t he after stolen documents?
\f1\i0  While Sarah rummaged the kitchen for a weapon\'97pots: gone; pans: absent; rolling pins, decent cutlery, pepper mills: all MIA\'97she muttered, \'93How do you people eat?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Agent Jones pinned Chanta to the couch with a knee. \'93Tell me where it is. Where is the disc?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta tried to gargle an answer. Jones eased his grip for speech.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I have no idea what you\'92re talking about!\'94 Chanta pleaded.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Jones battered Chanta\'92s head with the pistol butt, once, then again.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta protested with pained howls.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>In the pantry, Sarah found a case of beer. She hefted it chin-high and closed in behind Jones, intent as a cliff diver.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Stop,\'94 Agent Jones barked, swinging the pistol her way and cocking it in one motion.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Now she was staring down the barrel. On the couch, Chanta squirmed, twisted, managed to lever Agent Jones\'92 arm and hand\'97just enough. Reflexively, Jones pulled the trigger. The bullet tore a hole in the beer carton. Frothing liquid geysered out.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah shouted and brought the carton down hard on Agent Jones\'92 head. He went limp, collapsing over Chanta who now had to extricate himself from the unconscious agent, couch, and a spreading lake of lager.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah froze in the shock and beer spray.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta, grinning despite the pain, said, \'93You know you\'92ll have to pay for that beer.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>They tore down the fire stairs to the basement and out to Chanta\'92s van.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Where can we go?\'94 Chanta asked, as if expecting Sarah to snap her fingers and produce a convoy of bodyguards, the keys to a safe-house, or a rainbow made of cash.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I don\'92t know!\'94 Sarah grimaced.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>They dove into the van. Chanta floored it, demonstrating his total lack of racing driver credentials: tyres screaming, two wheels on kerbs.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93What the hell did you steal from these people?\'94 Sarah demanded, voice pitched on panic.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Nothing!\'94 Chanta spat, botching a turn, nearly connecting with a parked car.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93How about this disc?\'94 she shot back.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93What disc?\'94 Chanta said, sneaking glances at Sarah\'92s knees.\

\f2\i <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Not the time to ogle me,
\f1\i0  she thought bitterly. \'93Didn\'92t you hear? PAPER-boy slipped up. Stopped asking for documents, started demanding a disc.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Did he?\'94 Chanta feigned innocence. Sarah heard the shade in his voice.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Yes. I heard him. It\'92s obviously important. You really know nothing about it?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta\'92s blood ran cold. Sarah caught his hesitation.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93It\'92s nothing!\'94 Chanta insisted. \'93Just some old boot leather I picked up off a dead guy on the street, that\'92s all.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah squinted. \'93You stole a dead guy\'92s boot?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93No,\'94 Chanta said, but shame crept in, \'93just a bit of junk, okay? It\'92s still down there, I think, in the footwell.\'94 He nodded at her knees.\

\f2\i <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Oh. He was looking at the floor, not my legs.
\f1\i0  Sarah grimaced with a pang of disappointment \'97the male gaze gave her the ick, but a complete absence of it didn\'92t delight her either\'97and reached under the seat, fishing around with a level of suspicion she normally reserved for public toilets. She nearly missed it\'97just a scuffed disc, like a lost coaster\'97until it moved beneath her fingers. She retrieved it, turning it over under streetlight.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93This is what the fuss is about? Come on\'97wait, whoa.\'94 The leathery disc shimmered with a rainbow iridescence in her grip. \'93Did you see that?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93What?\'94 Chanta\'92s hands busy on the wheel.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93It\'85 flashed,\'94 Sarah said.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta risked a look; saw only a crumpled old disk of hide. \'93Yeah, thought it did that before. Just a trick of the light.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah turned it, twisted it, tried again. Then, on a whim, she tossed it spinning in the air.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>That instant, the van shuddered, pivoted over its front wheel, slammed onto the passenger side, and skidded sideways into a kebab cart. Tabouleh, sauces, red and white, splattered over the windshield.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah, on the collision side, was spared whiplash only because Chanta's flailing body landed on her.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You hit a pothole,\'94 Sarah said icily, enunciating each word like a death sentence.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Nope. I know these roads. No potholes back there.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Then what did you hit?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Dunno. Wombat?\'94 Chanta tried to grin, but his jaw throbbed. \'93C\'92mon, we gotta move.\'94 He crawled over her, stumbled through the mess in the back, and threw open the rear doors.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah followed, blinking kebab spray away. \'93Tax! I hate garlic sauce!\'94 Once outside, she checked out the wreck. No broken wheel, no bent axle, no explanation\'97just as if some angry sky-god had upended the van by hand.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93C\'92mon,\'94 they both called at each other almost in unison, Chanta already marching up the street, Sarah making for the opposite direction.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Where are you going?\'94 they said together, almost making a somewhat detuned chorus of it.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta waved for Sarah to explain first.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93There\'92s a hover-pad in the next block,\'94 she said.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Tax that!\'94 Chanta almost exploded. \'93I need a taxing drink.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Then I\'92ll book a hover-copt with a mini-bar,\'94 Sarah countered and made a face that said, 
\f2\i Don\'92t you dare doubt I can do it
\f1\i0 .\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Sure.\'94 Chanta shook his head. \'93Suppose it\'92ll let me board without electrocuting my armpits; how long before it alerts the authorities it\'92s got a Cloudborne on board?\'94 He shivered. There\'92s only so much a Cloudborne kid can loot from hover-copt wrecks before they swear they\'92ll never ride in one.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Despite their appalling safety record, there were aeronautical engineers who hailed the design of hover-copts as pure genius, but these were the kinds of geniuses who think, \'93Add vertigo, a midlife crisis, a forest of ailerons, and a battalion of thrust-adjusting fans; eventually, the Laws of Aerodynamics will take pity.\'94 To visualise a hover-copt, picture a dragonfly with an anxiety disorder, wings plucked off and replaced with a tangle of mismatched fans and blinking ailerons. Then mash it up with a Florida fan boat and bolt another enormous fan to its tail for propulsion. The result is every bit as jittery, overcompensating, and improbable as one imagines. Lift isn\'92t delivered by the smooth Bernoulli embrace known to proper aircraft. It isn\'92t so much achieved as negotiated; the fans only marginally more effective than a sigh attempting to keep a grand piano afloat. Each ascent feels less like flight and more like a confused emu flung from a trampoline in the general direction of \'93up\'94.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>In the air, a hover-copt doesn\'92t so much fly as bargain with gravity, flitting between platforms on a mixture of chaotic optimism and the faint aroma of fried circuits. Directional control is left up to a squabbling committee of mismatched fans, creating what engineers refer to as \'93probability turbulence\'94. Yaw, pitch, and roll are managed by desperate, generative artificial mediocrity circuits; after any given twitch, constant course corrections involve educated guesses as to where the craft will actually arrive several seconds later, a skill formerly reserved for jazz drummers and clairvoyant duck herders.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>In short: attaching a dizzying array of whirling fans to a metal box and calling it \'93flight\'94 is the kind of optimism only possible through a catastrophic misunderstanding of both air and boxes. Yet, with a great deal of luck, improvisation, and collective panic, most hover-copts stay aloft just long enough to deliver their passengers to their intended destinations, though not always with their internal organs in the order they were originally arranged.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah\'92s shoulders fell. \'93Where then?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93The Ghetto,\'94 Chanta said. \'93Hide out in a bar while we think about what to do next.\'94
